    <div type="book" osisID="3John">
      <title>3 John</title>
      <div type="chapter" osisID="3John.1">
        <title>3 John 1 — First-Century Reading</title>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="3John.1">
          <title>Context and Placement</title>
          <p><hi type="bold">The document's genre, identity, and occasion:</hi> This is a personal letter — the shortest document in the New Testament by word count — from a person who identifies himself only as "the elder" (ho presbyteros) to a man named Gaius. Unlike 1 John, which lacks all standard epistolary conventions (no named sender, no named recipient, no greeting, no closing), 3 John possesses every element of a genuine first-century personal letter: identified sender (by title), named recipient, opening health-wish, body with specific personal business, closing greetings, and a statement of intended visit. It is the most conventional letter in the Johannine corpus and the most firmly rooted in a specific, local, recoverable situation.</p>
          <p>The sender's self-designation — ho presbyteros, "the elder" — is identical to that of 2 John ("The elder to the elect lady and her children"). The word presbyteros in first-century usage carried two distinct ranges of meaning. First, it could denote an office-holder in a local community, an elder in the formal sense (as in Acts 14:23, where Paul and Barnabas appointed presbyterous in every city; or as in the Pastoral Epistles' instructions about the appointment and conduct of presbyteroi, 1 Timothy 5:17-19, Titus 1:5-9). Second, it could denote an older person whose authority derives not from office but from age, experience, and proximity to the original events — the surviving witness, the living link to the apostolic generation. Papias of Hierapolis (early second century, as preserved by Eusebius, Historia Ecclesiastica 3.39.4) distinguished between "the apostles" and "the elder John" (ho presbyteros Ioannes), treating the latter as a distinct figure whose testimony was valued precisely because he had heard the Lord's disciples directly. Whether this "elder" is the same as the author of 1 John and the Fourth Gospel, or a distinct figure within the same tradition, the text itself does not say. What the text does say is that this person exercises authority that extends beyond a single local assembly: he writes to individuals and communities, he sends and commends traveling workers, and he expects his directives to be honored. The self-designation "the elder" without further specification (not "an elder" or "an elder of such-and-such community") implies that the title was sufficient to identify him — he was the elder, the one his network knew by that designation alone.</p>
          <p>The vocabulary of this letter overlaps substantially with 1 John and 2 John: aletheia ("truth") appears six times in fifteen verses; agapetos ("beloved") appears four times; peripateo ("to walk") appears once, in the characteristic Johannine metaphorical sense of conducting one's life; martyreo ("to testify, to bear witness") appears four times; ergazomai ("to work") and its compounds appear in the description of the missionaries; tekna ("children") describes the elder's spiritual dependents; and the closing formula about speaking "face to face" (stoma pros stoma, literally "mouth to mouth") is identical to 2 John 12. The shared vocabulary, the shared self-designation, and the shared closing formula mark 2 John and 3 John as products of the same hand and the same period, and locate both within the broader tradition that produced 1 John and the Fourth Gospel.</p>
          <p>The occasion of the letter is a crisis of hospitality and authority. The elder has been sending traveling missionaries — brothers who go out "for the sake of the Name" (hyper tou onomatos) — to communities within his network. Gaius has received these missionaries and supported them. But a man named Diotrephes, who "loves to be first" (philoproteuon) in his community, has refused to receive the elder's emissaries, has spoken against the elder with malicious words, and has expelled from the community those who wished to receive the missionaries. The letter commends Gaius for his faithfulness, warns him about Diotrephes, and commends a third individual, Demetrius, who may be the carrier of this letter or the next missionary to arrive.</p>
          <p>The letter thus addresses a concrete ecclesiological conflict: the elder's recognized authority over a network of communities is being challenged by a local leader who asserts his own primacy. The letter is not primarily theological or doctrinal (unlike 1 John) but practical: it concerns the logistics and ethics of missionary support, the exercise of authority, and the marks of genuine versus counterfeit leadership. Yet even in addressing these practical matters, the letter deploys the same moral categories as 1 John — truth, love, walking, doing good versus doing evil, being "of God" versus not having "seen God" — and in doing so reveals that the Johannine tradition's diagnostic tests are not abstract theology but operating principles for real community life.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">The letter's structure and argument:</hi> The letter follows a clear three-part structure organized around three named individuals:</p>
          <list type="x-bullet">
            <item>Opening and commendation of Gaius (vv. 1-8): greeting, health-wish, joy at Gaius's walking in truth, commendation of his hospitality to traveling brothers, and exhortation to continue supporting the missionaries.</item>
            <item>Warning about Diotrephes (vv. 9-10): report of Diotrephes's refusal to accept the elder's authority, his slander against the elder, his refusal to receive the brothers, and his expulsion of those who do.</item>
            <item>The moral principle and commendation of Demetrius (vv. 11-12): the exhortation to imitate good rather than evil, grounded in the diagnostic formula that the one who does good is "of God" and the one who does evil has "not seen God"; the commendation of Demetrius as one whose testimony is universally positive.</item>
            <item>Closing (vv. 13-15): the convention of planned visit and final greetings.</item>
          </list>
          <p>The argument moves from positive example (Gaius) to negative example (Diotrephes) to moral principle (v. 11) to positive example (Demetrius). The structure is deliberate: Gaius is shown what faithfulness looks like in his own conduct, then shown what its opposite looks like in Diotrephes, then given the interpretive principle that connects conduct to identity ("of God" versus "not seen God"), and then given a concrete model (Demetrius) to follow. The letter is a practical application of the diagnostic method that 1 John developed in theological terms.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">Relationship to 1 and 2 John:</hi> Since this is a standalone letter and not a chapter within a larger work, there is no preceding or following chapter by the same author within the same document. The "surrounding argument" is provided by the other Johannine letters. 2 John addressed a community ("the elect lady and her children") and warned against receiving traveling teachers who deny that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh (2 John 7-11). 3 John addresses an individual and commends him for receiving traveling teachers who go out for the sake of the Name. The two letters are mirror images of the same practical problem: how should communities within the elder's network handle itinerant missionaries? 2 John warns against receiving the wrong ones; 3 John commends receiving the right ones. 1 John provides the theological framework within which both letters operate: its tests of right confession (believing that Jesus is the Christ, 1 John 2:22; confessing Jesus Christ as having come in the flesh, 1 John 4:2), right conduct (walking in the light, keeping the commandments, 1 John 1:7, 2:3-6), and right love (loving the brothers, 1 John 2:9-11, 3:14-18) are the criteria by which the traveling missionaries of 2 and 3 John are to be evaluated and by which the conduct of Gaius, Diotrephes, and Demetrius is assessed.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="3John.1.1-3John.1.2">
          <title>Verses 1-2: The Greeting and Health-Wish</title>
          <p>"The elder to the beloved Gaius, whom I love in truth. Beloved, I pray that in all things you may prosper and be in good health, just as your soul prospers" (vv. 1-2).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"The elder" (ho presbyteros).</hi> The sender identifies himself by title alone, without personal name. The article ho ("the") is definite — not "an elder" but "the elder." This implies that the designation was unambiguous within the relationship: Gaius knew who "the elder" was. As noted above, the same self-designation opens 2 John. The choice of presbyteros rather than apostolos ("apostle") or any other title locates the author's authority in his seniority and personal standing rather than in a formal commission. He leads by relationship and recognized stature, not by institutional title.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To the beloved Gaius" (Gaiō tō agapētō).</hi> The name Gaius (Latin Gaius, Greek Gaios) was one of the most common names in the Roman world. A Gaius appears as Paul's host in Corinth (Romans 16:23), a Gaius of Derbe traveled with Paul (Acts 20:4), and a Gaius was baptized by Paul at Corinth (1 Corinthians 1:14). The name's commonness means that identification of this Gaius with any of the Pauline figures is pure speculation unsupported by the text. The elder addresses Gaius as tō agapētō ("the beloved"), the same word used in 1 John to address the community (agapētoi, 1 John 2:7; 3:2, 21; 4:1, 7, 11). In the Johannine vocabulary, agapētos is not a casual term of affection but a relational marker: it identifies someone as belonging within the circle of love that the community shares. To call Gaius "beloved" is to locate him within the fellowship.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Whom I love in truth" (hon egō agapō en alētheia).</hi> The verb agapaō ("I love") is first person singular — personal, direct, from the elder himself. The prepositional phrase en alētheia ("in truth") can be read in two ways. First, as an adverb: "whom I truly love," i.e., with genuine, not feigned, love. Second, as a sphere: "whom I love within the sphere of truth," i.e., a love that operates within and is defined by the truth of the gospel. Both senses are present. In the Johannine vocabulary, "truth" (alētheia) is not merely sincerity but the content of the message about Jesus (cf. John 14:6, "I am the way, the truth, and the life"; John 17:17, "Your word is truth"; 1 John 1:6, "practice the truth"; 2 John 1-2, "whom I love in truth... because of the truth that abides in us"). The elder's love for Gaius is both genuine in its quality and grounded in the shared truth they hold. The identical phrase opens 2 John 1 ("whom I love in truth"), confirming the formulaic and theological character of the expression.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Beloved, I pray that in all things you may prosper and be in good health" (agapēte, peri pantōn euchomai se euodousthai kai hygiainein).</hi> The second use of agapētos (here in the vocative singular, agapēte) inaugurates the health-wish, a standard feature of Hellenistic letter writing. Papyrus letters from Roman Egypt routinely open with a prayer for the recipient's health and prosperity (e.g., P.Oxy. 292: "Above all I pray that you are well..."; BGU 423: "Before all things I pray to the gods for your health..."). The elder follows this convention exactly, which further confirms that this document is a genuine personal letter, not a theological treatise or homily cast in letter form.</p>
          <p>The verb euodousthai (from euodoō, "to prosper, to have a good journey, to succeed") is a present middle infinitive indicating ongoing well-being. In the LXX, the word appears in contexts of God granting success to a journey or an endeavor (Genesis 24:21, where Abraham's servant watches to see whether the Lord has made his journey successful, euodōken; Joshua 1:8, where meditation on the Torah causes one's way to prosper, euodōsei). The verb hygiainein ("to be in good health") is a straightforward health-wish. Together, the two verbs cover material prosperity and physical health — the whole of Gaius's temporal well-being.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Just as your soul prospers" (kathōs euodoutai sou hē psychē).</hi> The comparison is striking and has no parallel in the standard Hellenistic health-wish formula. The conventional letter would simply wish for the recipient's health; this letter adds a standard by which the wished-for health is measured: "just as your soul prospers." The word psychē in Johannine usage is not the post-Platonic "immortal soul" separable from the body, but the whole inner person — one's life, one's self, one's vital being (cf. John 10:11, 15, 17, where Jesus lays down his psychē for the sheep; John 12:27, "Now my soul is troubled"). The elder is saying: I know your inner life is flourishing; I pray that your outer circumstances may match it. This is not a conventional pleasantry. It is a specific assessment of Gaius's spiritual condition — the elder has received reports (as v. 3 will confirm) that Gaius is walking in truth, and on this basis pronounces that his soul is prospering. The health-wish thus becomes, in the elder's hands, a vehicle for pastoral commendation: Gaius's spiritual health is the standard by which his physical health is wished to measure up.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="3John.1.3-3John.1.4">
          <title>Verses 3-4: The Elder's Joy — Children Walking in Truth</title>
          <p>"For I rejoiced greatly when brothers came and testified to your truth, as you walk in truth. I have no greater joy than this: to hear that my children walk in truth" (vv. 3-4).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For I rejoiced greatly" (echarēn gar lian).</hi> The conjunction gar ("for") connects this verse to the health-wish: the elder can pray for Gaius's prosperity with confidence because he has received concrete reports about Gaius's faithfulness. The aorist echarēn ("I rejoiced") identifies a specific occasion of joy — the moment when the report arrived. The adverb lian ("greatly, exceedingly") intensifies the joy. This is not mild satisfaction but pronounced delight.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"When brothers came and testified to your truth" (erchomenōn adelphōn kai martyrountōn sou tē alētheia).</hi> The present participles erchomenōn ("coming") and martyrountōn ("testifying") are best understood as indicating repeated action: brothers have come on multiple occasions, and each time they have borne testimony to Gaius's faithfulness. The noun adelphōn ("brothers") designates fellow members of the community of faith — not biological siblings but the family created by shared birth from God (cf. 1 John 3:1-2, 14-16). These brothers are the traveling missionaries whose reception is the central issue of the letter. They have returned to the elder and given a report.</p>
          <p>The verb martyreō ("to testify, to bear witness") is a key Johannine term. In the Fourth Gospel, it describes the testimony of John the Baptist (John 1:7-8, 15, 32, 34), the testimony of Jesus himself (John 3:11, 32; 5:31; 8:14, 18), the testimony of the Father (John 5:32, 37; 8:18), the testimony of the works (John 5:36; 10:25), the testimony of the Scripture (John 5:39), the testimony of the Spirit (John 15:26), and the testimony of the beloved disciple (John 19:35; 21:24). In 1 John, it describes the triple testimony of the Spirit, the water, and the blood (1 John 5:6-8), God's testimony about his Son (1 John 5:9-11), and the community's own testimony (1 John 4:14). Testimony in the Johannine tradition is not casual endorsement but solemn attestation to truth. The brothers' testimony to Gaius's faithfulness carries this weight: they formally bear witness that Gaius lives in a manner consistent with the truth.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"To your truth" (sou tē alētheia).</hi> The dative tē alētheia is a dative of reference or respect: they testified concerning your truth, i.e., concerning the truth that characterizes you. "Your truth" does not mean a private truth that belongs to Gaius; it means the truth as it is embodied in Gaius's life. The genitive sou ("your") is possessive in the sense that the truth has taken hold in him and is visible in his conduct. The next clause makes this explicit.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"As you walk in truth" (kathōs sy en alētheia peripateis).</hi> The verb peripateō ("to walk") is the Johannine metaphor for the totality of one's conduct — the way one lives, not merely isolated acts. It appears throughout 1 John: "if we say we have fellowship with him and walk in the darkness" (1 John 1:6); "the one who says he abides in him ought to walk in the same manner as he walked" (1 John 2:6); "the one who says he is in the light and hates his brother is in the darkness... and walks in the darkness" (1 John 2:9, 11). Walking "in truth" (en alētheia) is the positive counterpart to walking "in darkness" (en tē skotia). In 2 John 4, the elder expressed the same joy in nearly identical words: "I was very glad to find some of your children walking in truth" (echarēn lian hoti heurēka ek tōn teknōn sou peripatountas en alētheia). The parallel confirms that "walking in truth" is a diagnostic marker in the elder's vocabulary: it identifies those who live in accordance with the message the community has received.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"I have no greater joy than this: to hear that my children walk in truth" (meizoteran toutōn ouk echō charan, hina akouō ta ema tekna en tē alētheia peripatounta).</hi> The statement reveals the elder's pastoral relationship to the people he serves. The word tekna ("children") identifies Gaius and others as the elder's spiritual offspring — people he has nurtured in the faith, for whose faithfulness he feels responsible. The same term appears in 1 John, where the author addresses the community as teknia ("little children," 1 John 2:1, 12, 28; 3:7, 18; 4:4; 5:21) and where the entire community is designated as God's tekna (1 John 3:1-2, 10; 5:2). The elder's use of "my children" (ta ema tekna) is not possessive in a controlling sense but relational: he identifies himself as the one who has begotten or nurtured them in the faith, and their faithfulness is the source of his deepest joy.</p>
          <p>The statement about joy echoes John 15:11 ("These things I have spoken to you so that my joy may be in you, and your joy may be made complete") and 1 John 1:4 ("These things we write so that our joy may be made complete"). Joy in the Johannine tradition is not subjective happiness but the relational completion that comes when the circle of fellowship — eyewitness, community, Father, Son — is intact and functioning. The elder's greatest joy is not personal achievement or institutional success but hearing that his children walk in truth. This is the same relational structure as 1 John 1:3-4: the eyewitness community's joy is completed when the audience participates in the fellowship.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="3John.1.5-3John.1.8">
          <title>Verses 5-8: Commendation and Exhortation — Hospitality to the Missionaries</title>
          <p>"Beloved, you are acting faithfully in whatever you do for the brothers, and strangers at that, who testified to your love before the church; and you will do well to send them on their way in a manner worthy of God. For they went out for the sake of the Name, accepting nothing from the Gentiles. Therefore we ought to support such men, so that we may be fellow workers with the truth" (vv. 5-8).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Beloved, you are acting faithfully in whatever you do for the brothers, and strangers at that" (agapēte, piston poieis ho ean ergasē eis tous adelphous kai touto xenous).</hi> The third use of agapēte ("beloved") marks a new section of direct address. The adjective piston ("faithful, reliable, trustworthy") is used adverbially: "you do a faithful thing," "you act faithfully." The phrase ho ean ergasē ("whatever you work/do") uses the subjunctive with the indefinite ean to cover all instances of Gaius's service: not merely one particular act but every act of hospitality he has performed. The verb ergazomai ("to work, to do, to perform") emphasizes active effort, not merely passive acceptance.</p>
          <p>The recipients of Gaius's service are described as tous adelphous ("the brothers") and then, with an intensifying addition, kai touto xenous ("and strangers at that" or "and these, strangers"). The word xenos ("stranger, foreigner, guest") indicates that the brothers Gaius has received were not members of his own local community. They were traveling missionaries unknown to him personally — strangers. The emphasis on their strangeness heightens the commendation: Gaius did not merely serve people he knew but extended hospitality to people he did not know, solely on the basis of their identity as brothers sent out for the work. In the world of first-century travel, hospitality to strangers was both a practical necessity (inns were expensive, unreliable, and often morally questionable) and a sacred obligation. Within Jewish tradition, the patriarch Abraham's reception of the three strangers at Mamre (Genesis 18:1-8 LXX) stood as the paradigmatic act of hospitality. Within the early communities of Jesus-followers, the Didache (a late first- or early second-century document) devotes specific instruction to how traveling apostles and prophets should be received, how long they should be hosted, and how to distinguish genuine from fraudulent missionaries (Didache 11-13). Gaius's hospitality places him squarely within this tradition.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Who testified to your love before the church" (hoi emartyresan sou tē agapē enōpion ekklēsias).</hi> The brothers who received Gaius's hospitality returned and testified — the aorist emartyresan ("they testified") points to a specific occasion — to his love (tē agapē) before the assembly (enōpion ekklēsias). The word ekklēsia here refers to the elder's home community or a gathering where the elder was present. The brothers' testimony is public and formal: they stood before the assembled community and bore witness to Gaius's love. The word agapē ("love") is the Johannine master-term for the ethical life of the community: "We know that we have passed out of death into life, because we love the brothers" (1 John 3:14); "let us not love with word or with tongue, but in deed and truth" (1 John 3:18). Gaius's hospitality is not merely practical assistance; it is an expression of the love that 1 John identifies as the defining mark of those who have passed from death to life. The brothers testify to his "love" (agapē), not merely to his "generosity" or "helpfulness" — the elder uses the theologically loaded term because Gaius's conduct is an instance of the love that the entire Johannine corpus holds as the supreme commandment.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And you will do well to send them on their way in a manner worthy of God" (hous kalōs poiēseis propempsas axiōs tou theou).</hi> The verb propempō ("to send forward, to send on one's way, to help on a journey") is a technical term in first-century Christian correspondence for the practice of equipping traveling missionaries with provisions, funds, and companionship for the next stage of their journey (cf. Romans 15:24, where Paul hopes to be "sent on his way" to Spain by the Roman community; 1 Corinthians 16:6, 11; 2 Corinthians 1:16; Titus 3:13). It means more than saying goodbye; it involves material support for the continued journey. The phrase axiōs tou theou ("in a manner worthy of God") sets the standard for this support: not merely adequately or generously, but worthily — in a manner that reflects the character of the God on whose behalf these missionaries travel. The standard is God himself. This echoes Paul's exhortation to the Thessalonians: "walk in a manner worthy of the God who calls you into his own kingdom and glory" (1 Thessalonians 2:12; cf. Colossians 1:10, Ephesians 4:1). The phrase implies that hospitality to God's messengers is a form of service to God, and that inadequate or grudging hospitality would be unworthy of God.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For they went out for the sake of the Name, accepting nothing from the Gentiles" (hyper gar tou onomatos exēlthon mēden lambanontes apo tōn ethnikōn).</hi> The conjunction gar ("for") explains why Gaius should support these missionaries: the nature of their mission demands it. They went out hyper tou onomatos — "for the sake of the Name." The Name is not specified, but in the context of the Johannine community, only one Name is in view: the name of Jesus, the Messiah, the Son of God. In the Fourth Gospel, the "name" of Jesus is the locus of salvation ("in his name," John 1:12; 20:31), prayer ("whatever you ask in my name," John 14:13-14; 15:16; 16:23-24, 26), and persecution ("they will do all these things to you on account of my name," John 15:21). In Acts, the apostles rejoice that they were "considered worthy to suffer dishonor for the Name" (Acts 5:41, hyper tou onomatos). The absolute use of "the Name" (tou onomatos) without further specification — not "the name of Jesus" or "the name of God" but simply "the Name" — occurs in a number of early Christian texts and reflects a reverence that treats the name as so singular that it needs no qualifier.</p>
          <p>The phrase mēden lambanontes apo tōn ethnikōn ("accepting nothing from the Gentiles" or "accepting nothing from the pagans") describes the missionaries' policy of refusing financial support from non-believers. The word ethnikōn (from ethnikos, "pertaining to the nations, Gentile, pagan") designates those outside the community of faith. The missionaries' refusal to accept support from outsiders serves multiple purposes. First, it preserves the integrity of their message: they do not want to appear as wandering philosophers or religious charlatans seeking profit from their teaching (cf. the Cynics and other itinerant teachers whose reputations were mixed). Second, it prevents any obligation to or dependence on people outside the community, which could compromise the content or direction of the mission. Third, it places the entire burden of support on the community of believers: if the missionaries will not accept help from outsiders, then insiders must provide for them. The elder's logic is clear: since these missionaries go out for the Name and refuse pagan support, the community of believers bears the exclusive responsibility for sustaining their work.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Therefore we ought to support such men, so that we may be fellow workers with the truth" (hēmeis oun opheilomen hypolambanein tous toioutous, hina synergoi ginōmetha tē alētheia).</hi> The verb opheilomen ("we ought, we are obligated") expresses moral obligation, not mere recommendation. The verb hypolambanein (from hypolambanō, "to take up, to receive, to support") means to take these missionaries under one's care, to receive them as guests and provide for their needs. The word is chosen carefully: it denotes active reception and ongoing support, not a one-time greeting at the door.</p>
          <p>The purpose clause hina synergoi ginōmetha tē alētheia ("so that we may become fellow workers with the truth") reveals the theological significance of hospitality. The noun synergos ("fellow worker, co-worker") appears throughout Paul's letters for those who share in the work of the gospel (Romans 16:3, 9, 21; 1 Corinthians 3:9; Philippians 2:25; 4:3; Colossians 4:11; Philemon 1, 24). Here the fellow-working is not with another person but with the truth itself (tē alētheia, dative of association). By supporting those who carry the truth, the community becomes a participant in the truth's mission. Hospitality is not merely a charitable act adjacent to the real work; it is the real work. Those who provide material support are as much "fellow workers with the truth" as those who travel and teach. The elder's formulation elevates practical hospitality to the level of partnership in the mission of God.</p>
          <p>This verse establishes the theological principle that governs the entire letter: supporting the work of truth is participation in the work of truth, and refusing to support it — as Diotrephes does — is opposition to it.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="3John.1.9-3John.1.10">
          <title>Verses 9-10: The Problem of Diotrephes</title>
          <p>"I wrote something to the church, but Diotrephes, who loves to be first among them, does not accept what we say. For this reason, if I come, I will call attention to his deeds which he does, unjustly accusing us with evil words; and not satisfied with this, he himself does not receive the brothers, and he forbids those who desire to do so and puts them out of the church" (vv. 9-10).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"I wrote something to the church" (egrapsa ti tē ekklēsia).</hi> The elder refers to a previous communication sent to the community where Diotrephes holds influence. The indefinite pronoun ti ("something") is either deliberately vague (the elder declines to summarize the content of the earlier letter) or dismissive (it was a brief communication, not a major document). Attempts to identify this "something" with 1 John, 2 John, or a now-lost letter are speculative. The text identifies neither the content of the communication nor the community to which it was sent. What matters for the argument is not what the elder wrote but what happened to it: Diotrephes rejected it.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But Diotrephes, who loves to be first among them, does not accept what we say" (all' ho philoproteuōn autōn Diotrephēs ouk epidechetai hēmas).</hi> The name Diotrephes (Diotrephēs, "nourished by Zeus") is a Greek name with pagan theophoric elements — common enough in the Greco-Roman world but notable in a Johannine context where "the Name" designates Jesus. The elder identifies Diotrephes by a single, devastating characterization: ho philoproteuōn autōn — "the one who loves to be first among them."</p>
          <p>The word philoproteuōn is a hapax legomenon — it appears nowhere else in the New Testament or in the LXX. It is a compound of phileō ("to love") and prōteuō ("to be first, to hold first place"). In Colossians 1:18, the verb prōteuō is used of Christ: "so that he himself will come to have first place (prōteuōn) in everything." The word the elder coins for Diotrephes takes a prerogative that belongs to the Messiah — holding first place — and attaches it to a human desire: Diotrephes loves to hold first place. The compound is sharp: it does not say Diotrephes happens to be the leader, or that he holds a position of authority; it says he loves to be first. His motivation is exposed: his exercise of authority is driven by desire for primacy, not by service. The genitive autōn ("among them") locates this desire within a specific community: Diotrephes wants to be first in his local assembly.</p>
          <p>The verb ouk epidechetai (from epidechomai, "to receive, to accept, to welcome") negates the reception: Diotrephes "does not accept us" (hēmas). The pronoun hēmas ("us") likely refers to the elder and his associates, or more broadly to the elder's authority and directives. Diotrephes's refusal is not merely disagreement; it is a rejection of the elder's recognized standing within the network of communities.</p>
          <p>The situation described here is an ecclesiological crisis, not (as in 1 John) a christological one. The elder does not accuse Diotrephes of false teaching about the identity of Jesus. He does not say Diotrephes denies that Jesus is the Christ or that he has come in the flesh. The charge is not doctrinal but behavioral and structural: Diotrephes loves primacy, rejects the elder's authority, slanders the elder, refuses hospitality to the traveling missionaries, and expels those in the community who wish to receive them. The crisis is about power, authority, and the practice of love — not about confessional content. This distinguishes the situation of 3 John from the situation of 1 John and 2 John, where the departure and deception involve christological denial.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"For this reason, if I come, I will call attention to his deeds which he does" (dia touto, ean elthō, hypomnēsō autou ta erga ha poiei).</hi> The conjunction dia touto ("for this reason") points forward to the list of charges. The conditional ean elthō ("if I come") indicates that the elder plans to visit but is not certain when. The verb hypomnēsō (from hypomimnēskō, "to remind, to bring to mind, to call to account") indicates that when the elder arrives, he will publicly address Diotrephes's behavior. This is not a private conversation; the context of the earlier letter "to the church" and the present letter addressed to Gaius (and through him, to others) suggests that the confrontation will be public. The elder will remind the community of what Diotrephes has done — bringing it to formal attention.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Unjustly accusing us with evil words" (logois ponērois phlyarōn hēmas).</hi> The verb phlyareō ("to talk nonsense, to bring unjust charges, to prate against") appears only here in the New Testament. In the broader Greek literature, it describes idle, malicious, or slanderous talk — speech that is both empty and harmful. The noun logois ponērois ("with evil words") identifies the content of Diotrephes's speech: his words about the elder are not merely critical but morally evil (ponēros, the same word used for "the evil one" in 1 John 2:13-14; 3:12; 5:18-19). Diotrephes has conducted a verbal campaign against the elder, attempting to discredit him in the community.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And not satisfied with this" (kai mē arkoumenos epi toutois).</hi> The participial phrase indicates that slander is not the extent of Diotrephes's opposition. He has gone further.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"He himself does not receive the brothers" (oute autos epidechetai tous adelphous).</hi> The same verb used for Diotrephes's refusal to accept the elder (epidechomai, v. 9) is now applied to his refusal to receive the traveling missionaries. The reflexive emphasis autos ("he himself") underlines personal responsibility: this is not a policy imposed from outside but Diotrephes's own decision. The "brothers" (tous adelphous) are the same traveling missionaries that Gaius has received (vv. 5-8) — those who go out for the sake of the Name. Diotrephes refuses them.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And he forbids those who desire to do so" (kai tous boulomenous kōlyei).</hi> Diotrephes extends his refusal beyond his own person: he actively prevents other members of the community from receiving the missionaries. The verb kōlyei ("he forbids, he prevents, he hinders") describes the exercise of coercive authority. Diotrephes has moved from personal refusal to institutional enforcement: he uses his position to block the hospitality of others.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And puts them out of the church" (kai ek tēs ekklēsias ekballei).</hi> The verb ekballō ("to cast out, to expel, to throw out") is forceful. In the Fourth Gospel, it appears when Jesus drives the merchants from the temple (John 2:15, exebalen) and when the man born blind is cast out of the synagogue (John 9:34-35, exebalon auton). The verb denotes authoritative removal — not a quiet distancing but a forcible expulsion. Diotrephes expels from the community (ek tēs ekklēsias, "out of the assembly") those who wish to receive the elder's missionaries.</p>
          <p>The picture that emerges is of a local leader who has consolidated power in a single community and is using that power to cut off the community from the wider Johannine network. He slanders the elder, refuses the elder's emissaries, prevents others from receiving them, and expels those who defy his prohibition. The elder's authority over this particular community has been effectively neutralized by Diotrephes's actions. The letter to Gaius is, in part, an attempt to maintain the connection with at least some individuals within or near that community.</p>
          <p>The parallel with 1 John 2:19 is instructive, though the situations differ. In 1 John, those who departed did so because they were "not of us" — their departure revealed their true allegiance. Here, Diotrephes does not depart; he stays and takes control. He does not leave the community; he reshapes it in his own image by expelling those who disagree. The danger in 1 John was secession; the danger in 3 John is domination. Both threaten the fellowship that the Johannine tradition defines as the community's reason for existing.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="3John.1.11-3John.1.12">
          <title>Verses 11-12: The Moral Principle and the Commendation of Demetrius</title>
          <p>"Beloved, do not imitate what is evil, but what is good. The one who does good is of God; the one who does evil has not seen God. Demetrius has received a good testimony from everyone, and from the truth itself; and we also testify, and you know that our testimony is true" (vv. 11-12).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Beloved, do not imitate what is evil, but what is good" (agapēte, mē mimou to kakon alla to agathon).</hi> The fourth use of agapēte ("beloved") introduces the letter's central moral instruction. The verb mimou (from mimeomai, "to imitate, to follow as a model, to emulate") is a present imperative: "do not make it your practice to imitate evil." The exhortation to imitation is common in early Christian literature (cf. 1 Corinthians 4:16, "I urge you, become imitators of me"; 1 Corinthians 11:1, "Be imitators of me, just as I also am of Christ"; Ephesians 5:1, "Be imitators of God"; 1 Thessalonians 1:6; 2:14; Hebrews 6:12; 13:7). The concept of imitation (mimēsis) was also central to Greek moral philosophy: Aristotle held that character is formed by habitually imitating virtuous models (Nicomachean Ethics 2.1). The instruction to imitate the good and avoid imitating the evil is not abstract ethics; in context, it refers directly to the examples just presented. Gaius has been shown a model of evil in Diotrephes and is about to be shown a model of good in Demetrius. The letter is structured so that the moral principle appears between the two concrete examples: do not imitate Diotrephes; imitate Demetrius (and, implicitly, continue in the pattern you have already established).</p>
          <p>The terms to kakon ("evil, what is evil") and to agathon ("good, what is good") are used here with the definite article, designating categories, not isolated acts. The elder is not saying "do not imitate this one bad thing" but "do not imitate evil as a category."</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"The one who does good is of God" (ho agathopoiōn ek tou theou estin).</hi> The compound verb agathopoieō ("to do good") appears also in 1 Peter 2:15, 20; 3:6, 17. The construction ek tou theou ("of God, from God") is the Johannine formula for origin and identity. In 1 John, ek tou theou distinguishes the children of God from the children of the devil: "the one who practices righteousness is of God (ek tou theou)" is the consistent logic (cf. 1 John 3:10, "By this the children of God and the children of the devil are obvious"; 1 John 4:4, "You are of God, little children"; 1 John 4:6, "We are of God; the one who knows God listens to us"; 1 John 4:7, "everyone who loves has been born of God"). The preposition ek indicates source, origin, and derivation: the one who does good has God as his source and therefore belongs to God. Conduct reveals origin. This is the same diagnostic principle that governs 1 John from start to finish: what a person does shows whose child he is.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"The one who does evil has not seen God" (ho kakopoiōn ouch heōraken ton theon).</hi> The compound verb kakopoieō ("to do evil") is the mirror opposite of agathopoieō. The perfect tense heōraken ("has seen") with the negative ouch makes a comprehensive denial: the one who does evil has never seen God — there has been no genuine encounter. In 1 John, "seeing" and "knowing" God are consistently linked to conduct: "the one who says 'I know him' and does not keep his commandments is a liar" (1 John 2:4); "no one who sins has seen him or knows him" (1 John 3:6). To "see" God in Johannine usage is not merely optical sight but the transformative encounter that produces changed conduct. The one who does evil demonstrates by that conduct that no such encounter has occurred.</p>
          <p>This verse is the theological hinge of the entire letter. It connects the specific, practical problem of hospitality and authority to the Johannine tradition's fundamental categories of identity. Diotrephes's conduct — his love of primacy, his slander, his refusal of hospitality, his expulsion of the faithful — is not merely administrative misbehavior. It is evidence, by the elder's diagnostic categories, that Diotrephes "has not seen God." The verdict is devastating: the man who has seized first place in the community is, by the criteria the Johannine tradition uses to distinguish children of God from children of the devil, one who has had no genuine encounter with God. Conversely, Gaius's faithful hospitality is evidence that he is "of God." The practical and the theological are inseparable.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Demetrius has received a good testimony from everyone" (Dēmētriō memartyretai hypo pantōn).</hi> A third named individual now appears. The name Demetrius (Dēmētrios, from Dēmētēr, the goddess Demeter) is another common Greco-Roman name. A silversmith named Demetrius in Ephesus opposed Paul in Acts 19:24-41, but there is no basis for connecting the two figures. The elder commends Demetrius with a threefold testimony.</p>
          <p>First, memartyretai hypo pantōn ("he has been testified to by everyone") — the perfect passive indicative of martyreō indicates that testimony about Demetrius has been given by multiple witnesses and stands as an established record. The "everyone" (pantōn) is likely the community of believers known to both the elder and Gaius, not literally every person in the world.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And from the truth itself" (kai hypo autēs tēs alētheias).</hi> The second witness is "the truth itself." This is a striking personification. The truth testifies to Demetrius. What this means concretely is that Demetrius's life is consistent with the truth — the truth of the gospel, the truth as the elder's community has received it. The testimony of truth is the testimony of the person's life measured against the standard of the message. A life that conforms to the truth is, in a sense, testified to by the truth: the truth recognizes its own, just as in 1 John 4:6, "the one who knows God listens to us." Demetrius's conduct matches the truth so closely that the truth itself can be cited as a witness.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"And we also testify, and you know that our testimony is true" (kai hēmeis de martyroumen, kai oidas hoti hē martyria hēmōn alēthēs estin).</hi> The third witness is the elder himself ("we," probably an epistolary plural or a reference to the elder and his circle). The phrase hē martyria hēmōn alēthēs estin ("our testimony is true") echoes a claim made in the Fourth Gospel about the beloved disciple's testimony: "This is the disciple who testifies about these things and has written these things, and we know that his testimony is true" (John 21:24; cf. John 19:35, "He who has seen has testified, and his testimony is true; and he knows that he tells the truth"). Whether the elder is the beloved disciple or belongs to the same circle, the claim to truthful testimony locates his witness within the same tradition. Gaius is told: you already know (oidas, perfect tense — settled knowledge) that our testimony is reliable. The elder does not need to establish his credibility with Gaius; Gaius already accepts it.</p>
          <p>The threefold testimony to Demetrius — from everyone, from the truth itself, and from the elder — functions as a letter of commendation. In the ancient world, letters of recommendation (systatikai epistolai, cf. 2 Corinthians 3:1) introduced a person to a community that did not know them and vouched for their character. Demetrius may well be the carrier of this letter, arriving at Gaius's door with the elder's written commendation in hand. The structure of the letter supports this reading: the elder has commended Gaius for receiving strangers (v. 5), warned about Diotrephes who refuses to receive (vv. 9-10), stated the principle that the one who does good is of God (v. 11), and now commends a specific individual whom Gaius should receive. The commendation of Demetrius is the letter's practical purpose: receive this man as you have received others.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="3John.1.13-3John.1.15">
          <title>Verses 13-15: The Closing</title>
          <p>"I had many things to write to you, but I do not wish to write to you with pen and ink; but I hope to see you shortly, and we will speak face to face. Peace to you. The friends greet you. Greet the friends by name" (vv. 13-15).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"I had many things to write to you, but I do not wish to write to you with pen and ink" (polla eichon grapsai soi, all' ou thelō dia melanos kai kalamou soi graphein).</hi> The elder indicates that this brief letter does not exhaust what he has to say. The imperfect eichon ("I had, I was having") suggests that the elder possessed the content but chose not to commit it to writing. The specific mention of "ink and pen" (melanos kai kalamou, literally "black [ink] and reed [pen]") — the physical tools of writing — emphasizes the medium's inadequacy for what the elder needs to communicate. The parallel in 2 John 12 is nearly exact: "Having many things to write to you, I did not want to do so with paper and ink" (dia chartou kai melanos). The repetition of this formula across the two letters confirms common authorship and a common situation: the elder has more to say than a letter can carry, and he intends to say it in person.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"But I hope to see you shortly, and we will speak face to face" (elpizō de eutheōs se idein, kai stoma pros stoma lalēsomen).</hi> The phrase stoma pros stoma ("mouth to mouth") is a Semitism corresponding to the Hebrew peh el-peh, which appears in Numbers 12:8 LXX, where God says of Moses: "mouth to mouth I speak to him" (stoma kata stoma lalēsō autō) — that is, directly, without mediation, without the obscuring veil of prophecy or vision. The elder's anticipation of face-to-face speech is not a casual travel note but a statement about the kind of communication he values: direct, personal, unmediated. The letter is a temporary expedient; the real communication will happen in person. This preference for direct presence over written word is consistent with the Johannine tradition's emphasis on physical, embodied encounter — the same tradition that insists its testimony is grounded in what was heard, seen, and touched (1 John 1:1-3).</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"Peace to you" (eirēnē soi).</hi> The benediction of peace (eirēnē, the Greek rendering of Hebrew shalom) is a standard Jewish greeting and farewell. Jesus used it as his post-resurrection greeting: "Peace be with you" (eirēnē hymin, John 20:19, 21, 26). It is not a wish for the absence of conflict but a blessing of wholeness, well-being, and right relationship with God.</p>
          <p><hi type="bold">"The friends greet you. Greet the friends by name" (aspazontai se hoi philoi. aspazou tous philous kat' onoma).</hi> The final sentence introduces a distinctive term: hoi philoi ("the friends"). This designation for fellow believers appears nowhere else in the New Testament epistles as a collective title for the community. In the Fourth Gospel, however, Jesus calls his disciples "friends" (philoi): "You are my friends if you do what I command you. No longer do I call you servants, because the servant does not know what his master is doing; but I have called you friends, because all things that I have heard from my Father I have made known to you" (John 15:14-15). The use of philoi as a community designation in 3 John may reflect the influence of this dominical saying: the members of the elder's network are "friends" because they have been brought into the intimate knowledge that Jesus shares with his own. The term is warm, personal, and non-hierarchical — distinct from "brothers" (adelphoi), "children" (tekna), or "saints" (hagioi). It suggests a community bound by personal affection and shared knowledge, not merely by institutional membership.</p>
          <p>The instruction kat' onoma ("by name") is telling: the elder wants Gaius to greet each friend individually, not with a generic collective greeting. The personal attention to individuals by name is consistent with the elder's pastoral character throughout the letter and with the Good Shepherd tradition of the Fourth Gospel: "He calls his own sheep by name and leads them out" (John 10:3). Each person in the community matters as a named individual, not merely as a member of a category.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="section" annotateType="commentary" annotateRef="3John.1">
          <title>Summary of the Letter's Argument</title>
          <p>The letter moves through a coherent sequence. The elder writes to Gaius, a trusted member of his network, to accomplish three things:</p>
          <p>First, to commend and encourage Gaius's practice of hospitality toward traveling missionaries (vv. 1-8). The elder grounds this commendation in the shared value of "truth" — walking in truth, working for the truth, becoming fellow workers with the truth. Gaius's hospitality is not a peripheral nicety but a form of participation in the truth's mission.</p>
          <p>Second, to warn Gaius about Diotrephes, who has seized control of a local community, rejected the elder's authority, slandered the elder, refused hospitality to the missionaries, and expelled those who wished to receive them (vv. 9-10). Diotrephes represents the opposite of everything Gaius embodies: where Gaius receives, Diotrephes refuses; where Gaius serves strangers, Diotrephes expels friends; where Gaius acts faithfully, Diotrephes acts from love of primacy.</p>
          <p>Third, to provide the interpretive key that connects conduct to identity: doing good marks a person as "of God"; doing evil marks a person as one who "has not seen God" (v. 11). This principle, drawn directly from the diagnostic method of 1 John, transforms the practical conflict over hospitality into a revelation of spiritual identity. The elder then commends Demetrius as a concrete model of the good to be imitated, supported by threefold testimony (vv. 11-12).</p>
          <p>The letter's closing (vv. 13-15) expresses the elder's preference for personal, embodied communication over written correspondence, echoing the Johannine tradition's valuation of direct, physical presence. The final greeting — "greet the friends by name" — enacts the personal, relational character of the community the elder serves.</p>
          <p>Throughout the letter, the vocabulary and categories of the broader Johannine tradition — truth, love, walking, testimony, doing good, being "of God," the friends — are applied not to abstract theological questions but to the concrete, practical realities of community life: who gets hosted, who gets expelled, whose authority is recognized, and what kind of conduct reveals genuine relationship with God. The letter demonstrates that the Johannine diagnostic tests are not theoretical constructs but working tools for navigating real ecclesiological crises.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
